('lesson-ohlc', 'Read one candle', 'A candle summarizes one interval using open, high, low and close. The body connects open and close; wicks reach the high and low. An up candle closes above its own open, not necessarily above the previous close.

• Body = abs(close - open). Range = high - low.
• Upper wick = high - max(open, close). Lower wick = min(open, close) - low.
• A small body means limited net movement, not necessarily low volume.
• Color schemes vary; use open and close values to determine direction.', 'foundation', '[]', 0),
('lesson-context', 'Locate the pattern', 'A pattern describes behavior rather than proving what comes next. Read the prior trend and surrounding price levels before assigning meaning.

• Support and resistance are areas of previous reactions; both can fail.
• Compare candle dimensions with recent volatility.
• Keep higher-timeframe context and nearby obstacles visible.
• Do not infer participant identity, intent or manipulation from a shape.', 'foundation', '[]', 1),
('lesson-confirmation', 'Separate candidates from confirmation', 'A candidate has the expected geometry. A confirmed example also satisfies a rule chosen beforehand, such as a completed close outside a boundary. Confirmation cannot eliminate false signals.

• Wait for the bar to finish before classifying its final shape.
• A wick beyond a level differs from a close beyond it.
• Retests are optional and may never occur.
• Historical labels must not depend on future information unavailable at signal time.', 'foundation', '[]', 2),
('lesson-risk', 'Plan hypothetical risk', 'An invalidation level marks where a scenario loses its basis. A stop order is an execution tool and can fill beyond the intended level during gaps or poor liquidity.

• For simple cash equities: units = floor(cash risk budget / abs(entry - stop)).
• Example only: a 500-unit cash risk budget and 5-unit price distance imply 100 shares before costs. This is arithmetic, not an allocation recommendation.
• Planned reward-to-risk = abs(target - entry) / abs(entry - stop). Reject zero distance and targets on the wrong side.
• Include spread, fees, slippage and gap risk. Contracts and leveraged products need multipliers and additional analysis.
• A candidate can be skipped when nearby opposing structure leaves too little room.', 'foundation', '[]', 3),
('lesson-evidence', 'Evaluate evidence', 'This library supplies no win rates and implements no trading strategy. A recognizable historical shape is not proof of positive returns.

• Define market, timeframe, entries, exits and costs before testing.
• Include failures and ambiguous examples.
• Keep development data separate from evaluation data.
• Report sample size, expectancy and drawdown alongside any win rate.
• Volume can add context where meaningful; it is not a universal guarantee.', 'foundation', '[]', 4),
('lesson-data', 'Check the market data', 'Candles and gaps depend on session settings, liquidity and data quality. Missing records or inconsistent corporate-action adjustments can create misleading formations.

• Use one instrument, timezone, session definition and adjustment policy per series.
• Reject non-finite values and records where high < max(open, close) or low > min(open, close).
• Handle zero-range candles before calculating ratios.
• All diagrams here use invented values and are not historical evidence.', 'foundation', '[]', 5),
('lesson-scanner', 'Before building automated detection', 'The supplied module is an educational content library, not an automatic detector. A production scanner needs precise definitions, appropriate data and independent evaluation.

• Parameterize body size, wick ratios, trend lookback, gaps and near-equality tolerances.
• Common ratios such as 2:1 are heuristics rather than universal thresholds.
• Distinguish candidate, confirmed and invalidated states.
• Do not add unsupported accuracy percentages or confidence scores.', 'foundation', '[]', 6),
('ascending-channel', 'Ascending Channel', 'The structure describes an upward trend.

Interpretation
trend · bullish

Context
During higher highs and higher lows.

Recognition
• Lower line joins higher lows.
• upper line tracks higher highs.
• approximately parallel spacing.

Confirmation to study
Lower-boundary break warns of weakness; upper-boundary break may indicate acceleration.

What weakens the scenario
For a bearish break thesis, recovery into channel and new highs weaken it.

Target framework
The shape supplies no reliable fixed target. In a hypothetical study, compare nearby opposing support or resistance with the planned risk distance.

Common mistakes and limitations
• The channel is not inherently bearish; a downside break can lead to consolidation.

Timeframe
Use completed candles from one consistent timeframe. Session boundaries, liquidity, costs and noise affect interpretation.

Not backtested; no success-rate claim.', 'chart', '[{"label":"Ascending channel: possible downward break","kind":"line","values":[80,100,86,106,92,112,98,89],"guides":[[0,80,6,98],[1,100,5,112]],"note":"Synthetic swing schematic. Final segment shows one possible breakout, not a forecast. Time spacing is simplified."}]', 100),
